package pitwall:tile@0.1.0;

/// Answers to agents' calls of the tile's show tools (agent-tools.md
/// §11.2–11.7). No permission: an answer describes only the agent's own
/// input, and show tools change nothing outside the tile.
///
/// Every tile can be opened by an agent with Pitwall's own
/// `<extension-id>__open`, and a manifest may declare show tools of kind
/// `show` or `present` (`tools` on its tile entry). A call reaches the
/// tile as `view-opened` (it opened a view) or `view-content` (it went to
/// the view that is open), with the call's id, the tool and the agent in
/// the `content` record. Pitwall has already checked the input against
/// the tool's schema.
///
/// A `present` call is all or nothing: input with problems changes
/// nothing, and the reply says why. Pitwall refuses, before the tile sees
/// it, a call that would replace an item the user changed since
/// (`user-edited`).
interface agent-tools {
  /// A problem in the agent's input, at a place in it when there is one
  /// (lines and columns from 1).
  record problem {
    line: option<u32>,
    column: option<u32>,
    message: string,
  }

  /// What a call did: a short text for the agent (at most 4 KiB, cut
  /// beyond; say what is shown, never the tile's data), optional JSON of
  /// the same, and the item id it was shown under (when the tile chose
  /// it).
  record tool-ok {
    text: string,
    json: option<string>,
    item: option<string>,
  }

  /// Why a call changed nothing: a message first, then each problem.
  record tool-error {
    message: string,
    problems: list<problem>,
  }

  /// A call's answer.
  variant tool-output {
    ok(tool-ok),
    error(tool-error),
  }

  /// Answer call `call` (from `content.call`). Only the first answer
  /// counts, and only while Pitwall still waits for it: during the
  /// `handle` that delivered the call. Without one, Pitwall answers with
  /// what opened and where.
  reply: func(call: u64, out: tool-output);

  /// The user changed what call `call` put in the tile (they typed in an
  /// agent's draft, moved its boxes). From now on that agent's calls with
  /// the same item are refused, before the tile sees them, with "The user
  /// changed this; show it under a new item id", so an agent never
  /// overwrites the user's edits.
  user-edited: func(call: u64);
}
